        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Tumor formation in humans or animals is a multi-step process. An early stage of cancer development is believed to be genomic instability (GI) which accelerates the mutation rate in the descendants of the cells surviving radiation exposure. A relationship between GI and tumor metastasis, as well as between GI and adaptation to drug treatment, has also been suggested. Evidence for GI induced from radiation exposure has been established in the last two decades. GI is defined as elevated or persistent genetic damages occurring many generations after the cells are exposed, and can be manifested in several biological consequences including mutation, apoptosis and damages in chromosomes. Genomic instability can be induced by both low- and high-LET radiation. Space radiation consists of a mixture of different types of ions covering a wide range of masses and energies. Its associated health risks to astronauts in flight are of special concern for prolonged space missions beyond the protective terrestrial magnetosphere. Before such missions can be undertaken, a much more detailed understanding of these biological effects is required to allow planning and implementation of protective countermeasures. This experiment investigated GI induced from exposures to energetic ions of the types and energies encountered during space flight. The overall goals of this study were (1) to determine the relationship between the quality and fluence of energetic charged particles and the induction of delayed damages in chromosomes, which is commonly referred to as chromosomal instability and (2) to understand the relationship between chromosomal instability and other events at the cellular and molecular level that play a role in the maintenance of genome integrity.
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
The investigators selected well-developed and widely used methodologies for quantification of GI. The three models included human T-lymphocytes collected from healthy subjects, and haemopoietic cells extracted from CBA/CaH and from C57BL/6J mice, which differ in radiosensitivity. The cells were exposed in vitro to protons, Si, and Fe ions which represent three of the most important particle types in space. Chromosomal instability was analyzed in both the total population of the cells surviving after multiple generations post irradiation (non-clonal assay), as well as in clonal populations derived from single cells surviving particle irradiation (clonal assay). The non-clonal assay is less labor intensive and allows analysis of samples exposed to particles of different types, energies, and doses, whereas the clonal assay was designed to tackle the question of genetic background influence on the induction of GI. Several techniques for chromosome analysis was used which include multi-color fluorescence in situ hybridization (mFISH) and the multi-color banding in situ hybridization (mBAND). Measurements of telomere length and expressions of genes involved in genomic stability were performed in cells from unstable, as well as stable, clones. The clonal expansion assay is arguably more suitable than the non-clonal assay for determining the correlation between chromosomal instability and other biological consequences. 
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
In this project, GI was investigated for both low- and high-LET radiation across different species and different cell types using both cell models and animal models. The degree of GI is apparently dependent on the quality of radiation, as well as on the biological systems. Consequently, the relative biological effectiveness (RBE) value can be different for different cell types and different endpoints. In this study, the RBE value for Fe ions can be high for initial chromosome aberrations in human lymphocytes. However, the value was found to be close to 1 when late damage was assessed. Early damage in the biological system does not necessarily predict residual damage, which is more closely related to late radiation risks such as cancer.
<p>
